 created this algorithm that if  you're not being voted on them in polls  you know they're going to start  surfacing your name more so you don't  feel left out what about other downsides  that we've seen from other apps like  data collection or the use of your  information yeah so this app based on  what one of the co-founders told me  they've made it very clear that privacy  is naturally one of their top concerns  and so even in the sign up process you  know you're prompted to like show your  contacts or your location so that you  can start pre-populating polls or get  matched to your high school faster but  you don't have to do that you can choose  not to do those things and then find  these things on your own and they've  also said if you do share your location  it's not associated with your account or  store word on their servers and another  feature that they've also implemented is  not allowing direct messages on the app  so if someone did try to bully you or  say you don't deserve to win best smile  or or whatever the poll was no one can  do that at least on the app the founders  of gas also had another app TBH can you  tell us a bit about that app and how  it's different from gas yes so five  years ago TBH was around similar concept  and climbed the App Store charts really  really quickly as well there were a few  different features like you could direct  message someone and so now one of the  co-founders of that app created gas and  he's done things in this app such as  creating that algorithm that surfaces  your name if you haven't been voted on  in polls and it has this in-app currency  that rewards you if you are voting for  people more so what happened with TBH  then why did they switch to gas  so TBH because of its popularity was  acquired by at the time the company we  know now is meta Facebook Inc and it was  alive and around for probably almost a  year after the acquisition and then in a  blog post Facebook said there was load  usage so they shut down the app  let's talk a little bit about  competition that they have because  there's been this boom of authentic or I  guess kind of more positive social media  apps being really popular with teens you  know be real is one locket is another so  can you give us a sense of what the  competition is going to be like for gas  and how sustainable its growth might be  given that I mean the competition really  depends on teens and what they're  looking for maybe they'll get sick and  tired of being  positive and too authentic and want to  you know move to another app where they  can be a little more real and share  their thoughts even if it's maybe a  little mean I think we are seeing the  shift though towards people moving  to these apps that are not curated that  they don't have any filters that it's  just kind of a nice little safe space  and you know it's a select group of  friends as well it's not everyone you  might know in school or in your life  what about in terms of business growth  for these apps I mean how are they going  to make money in the long term  that's the million dollar maybe billion  dollar question  but I think with many of these apps you  see them looking to introduce ads  um in the case of gas they do have  multiple features you can pay for and  you know that's some incremental revenue  and yeah that's the question do you  introduce ads and sort of add friction  to the experience or do you introduce  currency and subscriptions and then make  people pay for the service that was once  free and fun I wonder given the growth  that you're talking about in terms of  the social media market now the  experience that the gas Founders had  with their previous app TBH shutting  down after you know maybe becoming less  popular if we're going to see that kind  of ShakeOut happening in this social  media space soon are we going to see  that maybe some of these apps can't  survive or don't have the same following  or is there enough room for all of them  you know we've seen in this happen in  the past few years as well you know I'm  maybe dating myself a bit but I was  around during the days of Yik Yak and yo  and all these other apps that had  similar premises you know of sort of  connecting with your friends in a  one-off sort of  Anonymous type way and many of those  apps are now gone because you know to  your other question they couldn't  sustain a great business model so  unclear what will happen the market is  totally crazy and there's definitely  room for opportunity as we've seen of  tick tock and be real for apps to really  go viral but yeah the next question is  absolutely what will they do to maintain  that growth and of course pay themselves  make sure that they can sustain the  server capacity all these questions all  right we guess we'll just all have to  wait and see that's our personal Tech  reporter and Marielle quintera thanks  for joining us thank you  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

